# Do we need bees to eat? Four claims: true or myth?
subtitle: "Vanishing hives, wheat, chocolate: four claims about bees and our food, true or myth, and how much of our plate really needs pollinators."
tags: ["biology","food","data"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Save the bees, save the bread?\" stamp \"MYTH?\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Orus | a firm, confident quiz host; plain and kind","take":"lines"}

## Do we need bees to eat? Four claims: true or myth?
feedback:
    style: warm
    perfect: A beekeeper's eye.
    good: Most of the hive is with you.
    poor: These four fool almost everyone.
    none: The bees fooled you this time.
affirm: playful
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: How much of the world's food production is dependent on pollinators?
      authors: Hannah Ritchie, Our World in Data
      year: 2021
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/pollinator-dependence
      finding: 75 % of crop types depend on pollinators to some extent, but only about 35 % of global food production by weight comes from pollinator-dependent crops; without pollinators, production would fall about 5 % in high-income and 8 % in low-to-middle-income countries; cereals (wheat, rice, maize) are wind-pollinated; cocoa is fully dependent
    - id: fao
      title: Uptrend in global managed honey bee colonies and production based on a six-decade viewpoint, 1961–2017
      authors: B. J. Phiri, D. Fèvre, A. Hidano (Scientific Reports), from FAOSTAT
      year: 2022
      doi: 10.1038/s41598-022-25290-3
      finding: the number of managed honey bee colonies rose about 85 % between 1961 and 2017, driven mainly by Asia
    - id: midge
      title: 'Theobroma cacao: pollination'
      authors: Wikipedia, citing studies of Ceratopogonidae midges
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobroma_cacao
      finding: cacao flowers are pollinated mainly by tiny biting midges (Forcipomyia), not bees

